<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Reutlingen</h1><p class="st-meta">1240 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Reutlingen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Swabian imperial city below the Jura that beat the Count of Württemberg in 1377 and was one of two cities to sign the Augsburg Confession in 1530.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Reutlingen grew at the foot of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achalm">Achalm</a> hill on the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a>, and was fortified and granted town rights by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> around 1240, becoming an imperial city. Its walls soon had to resist the counts of Württemberg, whose territory surrounded it.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">Guilds</a> came to dominate the council, an unusual feature among Swabian cities. In 1377 the townsmen defeated Ulrich of Württemberg at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-reutlingen">Battle of Reutlingen</a>, a high point of the Swabian city leagues. Cloth and leather were its main trades. Under the preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthaus-alber">Matthäus Alber</a> it adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 1520s, and in 1530 it was one of only two cities to sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a>, with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-nuremberg">Nuremberg</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>A great fire in 1726 destroyed much of the town. Reutlingen lost its freedom in 1802, when it was annexed by Württemberg, and it is now a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>; the Gothic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marienkirche-reutlingen">Marienkirche</a> survived the fire.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1240 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.38%;width:11.18%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1240 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Reutlingen</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reutlingen</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1521&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-reutlingen" aria-label="Free Imperial City of Reutlingen on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4894 483 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Free Imperial City of Reutlingen lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5214 739L5229 694M5214 739L5212 730"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5212 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5214 739h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5214 739h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Reutlingen. <a href="/atlas?year=1521&amp;focus=free-imperial-city-of-reutlingen">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
